\description{
The main function of the NCA part of the package. It reads the dataset, cuts
every subject into dosing intervals, analyses every interval and summarises
the parameters, all with settings you control through \code{\link[=hp_nca_options]{hp_nca_options()}}.
Oral, intravenous bolus and infusion dosing are supported, as are single doses,
repeated doses and steady state.
}
\details{
The analysis follows WinNonlin practice. For each profile it reports:
\itemize{
\item peak and trough: \code{CMAX}, \code{TMAX}, \code{CLAST}, \code{TLAST}, \code{TLAG} (extravascular
single doses), \code{C0} (intravenous bolus) and, for dosing intervals,
\code{CMIN} and \code{TMIN};
\item terminal phase: \code{LAMZ}, \code{THALF}, the number of points, the window and the
R-squared and adjusted R-squared of the regression, and \code{CLAST_PRED};
\item areas: \code{AUCLAST}, \code{AUCINF_OBS}, \code{AUCINF_PRED}, the percent extrapolated
(\code{AUC_PCT_EXTRAP_OBS}, \code{AUC_PCT_EXTRAP_PRED}), \code{AUMCLAST}, \code{AUMCINF_OBS},
\code{AUMCINF_PRED}, \code{MRTLAST}, \code{MRTINF_OBS}, \code{MRTINF_PRED} and partial areas
such as \code{AUC_0_12};
\item clearance and volume: \code{CL_OBS}, \code{VZ_OBS}, \code{VSS_OBS} (and \verb{_PRED}) for
intravenous doses, \code{CL_F_OBS} and \code{VZ_F_OBS} (and \verb{_PRED}) for
extravascular doses;
\item over the dosing interval: \code{AUCTAU}, \code{AUMCTAU}, \code{CTAU}, \code{CAVG},
\code{FLUCT_PCT}, \code{SWING}, \code{PTR}, and at steady state \code{CL_SS} (\code{CL_F_SS}),
\code{VZ_SS} (\code{VZ_F_SS}), \code{MRT_SS}, \code{VSS_SS}, and the accumulation ratios
\code{RACC_PRED}, \code{RACC_AUC} and \code{RACC_CMAX};
\item dose-normalised values \code{CMAX_D}, \code{AUCLAST_D} and \code{AUCINF_D}.
}

Run \code{\link[=hp_nca_parameters]{hp_nca_parameters()}} for the full dictionary. Quality flags in the
\code{FLAGS} column mark profiles whose terminal phase is poor or whose extrapolated
area is large; see \code{\link[=hp_nca_analyse]{hp_nca_analyse()}}.

The areas, moments and terminal regressions are computed in C++, so a
study of thousands of profiles runs in seconds.

State the \code{route} explicitly whenever you know it; \code{"auto"} is a
convenience that guesses from the shape of the data (see \code{\link[=hp_nca_read]{hp_nca_read()}}).
}
\examples{
d <- hp_read_data(hp_example_file())

# a standard analysis of an oral study; concentrations below 0.5 are BLQ
r <- hp_nca(d, route = "oral", lloq = 0.5)
r
head(r$parameters[, c("ID", "CMAX", "TMAX", "AUCLAST", "AUCINF_OBS", "THALF")])

# by dose group, with the linear trapezoid rule and a partial area
opt <- hp_nca_options(auc_method = "linear", lloq = 0.5, partial = list(c(0, 12)))
r2 <- hp_nca(d, options = opt, route = "oral", groups = "DOSEGRP")
r2$summary[r2$summary$PARAMETER \%in\% c("CMAX", "AUC_0_12"),
           c("STRATUM", "PARAMETER", "N", "GEOMEAN", "GEOCV_PCT")]

# profiles that were not analysed, and why
r$parameters$SKIPPED[r$parameters$SKIPPED != ""]
}
